l partners", "IV drug use", "Antibiotic use prior to rash (allergic reaction consideration)"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Allergic/Dermatologic Reaction with Cutaneous Manifestations", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.38795094165340993, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["The consistent timing of rash post-sex and recent antibiotic use moderately supports an allergic/dermatologic reaction (B1). Low-grade fever (37.5°C) and recurrent post-coital rash provide strong support for atypical STI explanations (B2.4) and some support for gonorrhea (B2.3). Lack of mucocutaneous lesion description and needle stick history weakens atypical STI (B2.4). Subacute pattern and absence of herpes-like features (vesicles, pain) marginally weakens HSV-2 (B2.2). IV drug use remains a red herring with insufficient evidence to affect B3.", "The temporal link between antibiotics and rash moderately supports an allergic/dermatologic reaction (B1). Recurrent post-coital rash and fever also add some support for gonorrhea (B2.3) and strong support for atypical STI (B2.4), but the lack of characteristic HSV-2 vesicles slightly reduces its likelihood (B2.2)."], "evidence_against": ["The absence of expected drug eruption features in the uncharacterized Figure A weakens the allergic reaction hypothesis (B1) but does not directly affect the likelihood of systemic infections (B2)."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Has the patient used any new medications or substances recently?", "Is there a history of previous allergic reactions to medications?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Skin prick/test for drug or contact allergy", "Review of medication timeline vs rash onset"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.85, "interrupt_relevance": 0.4, "l